<% content_for :title, "ADHD | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="toolbox-adhd-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="toolbox-adhd-hero">
    <div class="toolbox-adhd-hero-content">

      <p class="toolbox-adhd-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / ADHD
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>YOUR BRAIN</span>
        <span>HAS OTHER</span>
        <span>"BIG" PLANS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="toolbox-adhd-intro">
        DISTRACTED. OVERWHELMED.<br>
        BORED. RESTLESS.<br>
        TRYING TO HOLD IT ALL TOGETHER.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR VERSION -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-pink"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-1-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      01 / YOUR VERSION
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-1-title">
        IT'S MORE<br>
        THAN<br>
        DISTRACTION.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Your ADHD shows up in the details of your actual day:
        you start multiple projects and sometimes struggle to
        finish them. You zone out or daydream. You lose track of
        conversations, have trouble staying on task, get distracted,
        and make mistakes because your attention wandered.
      </p>

      <p>
        You forget parts of your daily routine and bills you meant
        to pay. You misplace things. Your brain gets foggy or
        overloaded. Sometimes you know exactly what needs to happen
        and still can't get yourself to start.
      </p>

      <p>
        And you're not sitting perfectly still while all this
        happens. You fidget. You feel restless inside. You pace
        while you're on the phone.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-callout">
        <p>
          THIS IS WHAT YOUR ADHD<br>
          LOOKS LIKE IN YOUR LIFE.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 / ATTENTION AND CONVERSATIONS -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-2-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      02 / LOSING THE THREAD
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-2-title">
        I'M LISTENING.<br>
        WAIT—WHAT<br>
        DID YOU SAY?
      </h2>

      <p>
        You can be looking at someone and still lose the thread
        of what they're saying. Your mind wanders. A thought pulls
        you away. You realize you heard their voice but didn't
        hold onto the words.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes there is a delay between hearing something and
        making sense of it. You may need a moment, a repeat, or a
        different explanation before the words click. That pause
        doesn't mean you don't care about the person or the
        conversation.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to fake understanding to keep things moving.
        You can say, “I lost the last part—can you say it again?”
        or “Give me a second to process that.”
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-tool-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>Ask for the last sentence again instead of guessing.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>Write down names, dates, or action items while they're being discussed.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>Repeat back what you heard when the details matter.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR PHONE -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-purple"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-3-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      03 / YOUR EXTERNAL BRAIN
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-3-title">
        YOUR PHONE<br>
        IS HOLDING<br>
        THE LIST.
      </h2>

      <p>
        You use your phone for everything because remembering
        everything internally is unreliable. Your calendar, alarms,
        notes, reminders, and lists aren't extra. They're part of
        how you keep appointments, routines, projects, and bills
        from disappearing from view.
      </p>

      <p>
        The trick is getting the reminder into the phone before
        the thought vanishes. If you tell yourself, “I'll put that
        in later,” later may not remember what you meant.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-tool-box">

        <h3>CAPTURE IT WHILE IT'S HERE.</h3>

        <p>
          Put the appointment in the calendar. Add a reminder
          for the bill. Write the thought in one place you
          actually check. Make the reminder specific enough
          that you know what to do when it appears.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your phone isn't proof you can't remember.
          It's how you make remembering less dependent
          on memory.
        </p>

      </div>

      <p>
        For recurring routines, use the same cue each time:
        an alarm, a checklist, or a reminder attached to
        something you already do. The system can hold the
        sequence when your brain doesn't.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 / PROJECTS AND EXECUTIVE DYSFUNCTION -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-black"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-4-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      04 / STARTING AND FINISHING
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-4-title">
        TWELVE<br>
        OPEN PROJECTS.<br>
        WHERE'S THE END?
      </h2>

      <p>
        Starting a project can feel electric. There's a new idea,
        a new plan, a fresh burst of possibility. Then the work
        gets repetitive, complicated, or less immediately exciting.
        Another idea shows up. Now several projects are competing
        for your attention, and finishing any one of them feels
        harder than starting the next.
      </p>

      <p>
        Executive dysfunction can make the next step hard to find,
        even when you care about the outcome. The project stays
        huge and vague in your head. You might switch between
        tasks, lose your place, or put off returning because
        you can't remember where you left off.
      </p>

      <p>
        Give each project a visible “next action” and a “where
        I stopped” note. When you come back, you won't have to
        reconstruct the whole plan from scratch.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-tool-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>Keep one list of active projects so every idea doesn't become today's priority.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>Write the next physical action, like “open the file” or “email the person.”</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>Before stopping, leave yourself a note about what you did and what comes next.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>Choose what you're finishing before you start adding more.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 / RESTLESSNESS -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-pink"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-5-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      05 / RESTLESSNESS
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-5-title">
        THE BODY<br>
        KEEPS<br>
        MOVING.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Sometimes the restlessness is physical: fidgeting,
        shifting, getting up, walking around. Sometimes it feels
        like your body has to move before your mind can settle.
      </p>

      <p>
        You pace while you're on the phone. You may get up without
        deciding to. Sitting still can take effort, especially
        when your thoughts are already moving between things.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't need to turn every movement into another thing
        you're doing wrong. Notice what helps you stay present:
        pacing during the call, holding something, standing up,
        or taking a short movement break.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-callout">
        <p>
          MOVEMENT CAN BE PART<br>
          OF HOW YOU PAY ATTENTION.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 / EMOTIONAL SENSITIVITY -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-6-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      06 / EMOTIONAL SENSITIVITY
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-6-title">
        IT HITS<br>
        HARD.<br>
        IT STICKS.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Your emotions can feel intense and immediate. A criticism,
        a change in someone's tone, or the possibility that somebody
        is upset with you can take up your whole attention. You may
        replay what happened and search for what you did wrong.
      </p>

      <p>
        You call some of this RSD. Rejection or the fear of rejection
        can feel enormous, even when you're still trying to figure
        out what the other person meant. The feeling deserves
        attention; it doesn't automatically tell you what they
        think or what you have to do next.
      </p>

      <p>
        When you're activated, try separating what happened from
        what you fear it means. You don't have to send the apology,
        agree to the request, or fix the whole relationship in
        the same minute.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-tool-box">

        <h3>GIVE THE FEELING A MINUTE.</h3>

        <p>
          “I feel rejected right now. I don't know yet what
          they meant. I can ask, wait, or think about it again
          when this doesn't feel so immediate.”
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 / PEOPLE-PLEASING -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-purple"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-7-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      07 / SAYING YES
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-7-title">
        SURE, I CAN<br>
        DO THAT.<br>
        WAIT. CAN I?
      </h2>

      <p>
        You can agree to a request before you've checked your
        time, energy, or other commitments. Saying yes can feel
        easier than risking conflict, disappointing somebody,
        or wondering whether they'll reject you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then the yes becomes another responsibility to remember.
        You may overcommit, feel overwhelmed, and criticize
        yourself when you can't keep up with everything you
        promised.
      </p>

      <p>
        You are allowed to pause before answering. A delay gives
        you time to check what the request actually involves and
        whether you can take it on.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-tool-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>“Let me check my calendar before I say yes.”</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>“I can't commit to that right now. I'll get back to you.”</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>“I can help with this part, but I can't take on the whole thing.”</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        A pause isn't a rejection of the other person.
        It's a chance to make an honest commitment.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 08 / SHAME AND OVERWORKING -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-black"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-8-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      08 / SHAME AND OVERCOMPENSATING
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-8-title">
        LAZY?<br>
        OR TRYING<br>
        TO HIDE THE CHAOS?
      </h2>

      <p>
        Forgetfulness, unfinished projects, and difficulty getting
        started can feed a persistent feeling of inadequacy.
        You may look at what you haven't done and decide it means
        you're lazy, unreliable, or falling behind everyone else.
      </p>

      <p>
        Perfectionism and overworking can become ways to compensate.
        You push yourself to do everything exactly right so nobody
        sees the missed details, the overwhelm, or how much effort
        it took to get started. You may work past the point of
        exhaustion because stopping feels like proof you didn't
        try hard enough.
      </p>

      <p>
        The shame is real, but it doesn't make the labels true.
        You can take responsibility for what needs doing without
        using self-punishment as your project management system.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-callout">
        <p>
          STRUGGLING TO START<br>
          DOESN'T MEAN YOU DON'T CARE.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Notice when “I need a workable plan” turns into “I need
        to prove I'm not lazy.” Those are different problems.
        You deserve a system that helps you function, not one
        that demands you overwork to earn your own respect.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 09 / ADHD AND BIPOLAR -->
  <section class="adhd-section adhd-section-pink"
           aria-labelledby="adhd-section-9-title">

    <div class="adhd-section-number">
      09 / ADHD AND BIPOLAR
    </div>

    <div class="adhd-section-content">

      <h2 id="adhd-section-9-title">
        KNOW YOUR<br>
        USUAL.<br>
        NOTICE A SHIFT.
      </h2>

      <p>
        ADHD is part of your ongoing pattern. Bipolar episodes
        can bring changes from your usual baseline. Since attention,
        activity, restlessness, and impulsivity can show up in
        more than one way, one rough day doesn't tell you the
        whole story.
      </p>

      <p>
        What matters is the pattern you notice over time:
        what is usual for you, what has changed, and whether
        sleep, energy, mood, judgment, or behavior is shifting
        along with it.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to solve the diagnostic puzzle by yourself.
        Keep track of what feels different and bring it to your
        psych, especially if the change is affecting
        your decisions or daily life.
      </p>

      <div class="adhd-callout">
        <p>
          YOUR BASELINE IS USEFUL INFORMATION.<br>
          SO IS A CHANGE FROM IT.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="adhd-ending">

    <p class="adhd-ending-small">
      THIS IS YOUR BRAIN. BUILD AROUND WHAT YOU KNOW ABOUT IT.
    </p>

    <h2>
      USE THE<br>
      REMINDERS.<br>
      KEEP THE<br>
      WEIRD SYSTEM.
    </h2>

    <p>
      Your phone can remember the bill. Your notes can hold the
      next step. You can pace through the call, ask someone to
      repeat themselves, and take a minute before you say yes.
      You don't have to hide how much effort this takes.
    </p>

    <%= link_to "← BACK TO TOOLBOX",
        toolbox_path,
        class: "adhd-back-link" %>

  </section>

</main>